TI  - Airway involvement in sarcoidosis.
PG  - 1371-1380
LID - S0012-3692(09)60695-4 [pii]
LID - 10.1378/chest.08-2569 [doi]
AB  - Sarcoidosis is a common disease and affects the respiratory system in > 90% of 
      cases, most commonly the intrathoracic lymph nodes and the respiratory 
      parenchyma. Less commonly, the airways are involved, and the disease is 
      manifested as mucosal erythema, edema, granularity and cobblestoning, plaques, 
      nodules, and bronchial stenosis, airway distortion, traction bronchiectasis, and 
      bronchiolitis. Airway involvement may lead to airflow limitation. Involvement of 
      oral, nasal, and pharyngeal mucosa may cause hoarseness, dysphagia, laryngeal 
      paralysis, and upper airway obstruction. Airway symptoms are important indicators 
      of airway involvement in sarcoidosis. Pulmonary function testing, radiologic 
      imaging, and bronchoscopy occupy a significant role in the diagnosis and 
      management of airway involvement in patients with sarcoidosis.
