TI  - Incidence of complications in bronchoscopy. Multicentre prospective study of 
      20,986 bronchoscopies.
PG  - 8-14
AB  - AIM: To evaluate the frequency of complications in bronchoscopy from data 
      compiled between 1/2/2002 to 1/2/2003. MATERIALS AND METHODS: Nineteen Italian 
      centres of thoracic endoscopy participated in the study, for a total of 20,986 
      bronchoscopies (FBS), including 10,658 explorative bronchoscopies (EB) (50.79%), 
      5,520 bronchial biopsies (BB) (26.30%), 1,660 transbronchial biopsies (TBB) 
      (7.91%), 1,127 broncho-alveolar lavages (BAL) (5.37%), 930 transbronchial 
      needle-aspirates (TBNA) (4.43%), 1.091 therapeutic bronchoscopies (TB), 
      comprising ND-YAG Laser, argon-plasma, electrocautery knife, stent insertion 
      (5.20%). 82.4% of the procedures involved the use of a flexible bronchoscope, 
      16.3% were carried out using a rigid bronchoscope and 1.3% using the mixed 
      technique. RESULTS: The total number of complications recorded was 227 (1.08% of 
      the cases examined), including 20 (0.09%) during local anesthesia and 
      pre-medication, 195 (0.92%) during the endoscopic procedures and 12 (0.05%) in 
      the two hours following FBS. The total number of deaths was 4 (0.02%), due to 
      cardiac arrest, pulmonary edema, delayed respiratory failure and shock in 
      pre-medication, respectively. 68.28% of the complications were treated medically, 
      25.99% by means of endoscopy and 5.72% with surgery. The healing percentage was 
      98.2%. CONCLUSIONS: This study has shown that bronchoscopy is a safe method with 
      low incidence of mortality and complications. The preparation, experience and 
      continuous training of the operators of the medical and nursing team seem to play 
      a fundamental role in reducing the incidence of complications at least in certain 
      procedures such as BB and TBB.
