VI  - 62
IP  - 5-6
DP  - 2009 May-Jun
TI  - [Biliobronchial fistula due to hydatidosis of the liver and choledocholithiasis].
PG  - 281-4
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Biliobronchial fistula (BBF) is an abnormal communication between 
      the biliary system and bronchial tree due to trauma, hemiheptectomy benign 
      biliary stricture with cholangitis and necrotic hepatic infections such as 
      hydatid disease. Intratoracic rupture of a hydatis cyst of the liver is a rare 
      but severe complication with an incidence about 1% and mortality rate about 10%. 
      CASE REPORT: A 50-year-old male patient presented with cough, episodes of biliary 
      expectoration and haemoptysis followed with fever, fatigue and dyspnea. The 
      diagnosis was set by echosonography, Chest x-ray finding, CT scan, bronchoscope 
      aspirates while bronchography did not show results. The surgery was done through 
      right subcostal laparotomy. A hydatid cyst was found in the right liver lobe its 
      diameter being 10 cm, fixed with diaphragm. Intraoperative cholangiography 
      revealed choledocholithiasis (two calculi), dilatation of bile ducts and 
      communication of right hepatic duct with the irregular liver cavity as well as 
      with the bronchi of the right lower pulmonary lobe. Cholecystectomy, 
      choledocholitotomy with drainage lavage of bile ducts pericystectomy and cyst 
      evacuation was performed, followed by diaphragm necrosectomy. The postoperative 
      period and control cholangiography through T drain were normal. DISCUSSION: The 
      surgical treatment has five goals: to treat the liver cyst, secure free biliary 
      drainage, perform hepatodiaphragmatic disconection, solve intratoracic lesion and 
      restore the diaphragm. We believe these goals could be achieved through abdominal 
      approach except for irreversible bronchiectasis, intratoracal collection and 
      chronic pulmonary sepsis. CONCLUSION: In any case, a surgery still remains the 
      treatment of choice in cases of echinococcosis. Most cases can be solved by 
      laparotomy.
