TI  - [Giant solitary fibrous tumor of the pleura--case report].
PG  - 97-102
AB  - Solitary fibrous tumors of the pleura are rare malignant pathological findings, 
      accounting for only 5% of all pleural neoplasms. Clinical manifestations are very 
      unspecific and over 50% of the cases are asymptomatic. The commonest clinical 
      symptoms include cough, pains, dyspnoea, fever and weight loss. The recommended 
      diagnostic methods include chest x-ray, CT and MRI imaging and positrone emission 
      tomography. The CT-assissted puction biopsy yield is less than 50%. The authors 
      present a case review of a 58-year-old female with a history of dyspnoea. Her 
      chest x-ray and CT examination suggested a large cystoid lesion in the right 
      hemithorax, with extensive dystelectasis of the right lung lobe. Functional 
      pulmonary examination confirmed severe gas transfer dysfunction, with diffusion 
      lung capacity of 47% of the normal. Stenosis of the right middle and lower lobar 
      bronchus resulting from extramusral pressure was detected on bronchoscopy. The 
      authors performed right-sided thoracotomy and removed an encapsulated solid tumor 
      measuring 24 x 16 x 13.5 cm and weighting 2850 grams from the thoracic cavity. 
      Benign solitary fibrous tumor was diagnosed on histology. The postoperative 
      course was complicated by bleeding into the pleural cavity, which was managed 
      conservatively and did not require subsequent surgical revision. The patient was 
      released for home care in a good condition. The aim of the study is to draw 
      attention to this tumorous disorder of the pleura - the solitary fibrous tumor.
