DP  - 2010 May
TI  - Chronic Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection in a child after renal transplantation.
PG  - E26-9
LID - 10.1111/j.1399-3046.2009.01130.x [doi]
AB  - Mycoplasma pneumoniae has rarely been reported in renal transplant recipients. We 
      present the case of a 10-yr-old boy with a six-month history of chronic cough, 
      recurrent pyrexia, and weight loss three yr after RTx. The patient's 
      post-transplant course was complicated by recurrence of NS that resolved with 
      plasmapheresis and PTLD, which was successfully treated with an anti-CD20 
      monoclonal antibody. Chest X-ray showed a round mass-like lesion in the left 
      upper lobe; MRT, PET, and bronchoscopy ruled out a PTLD. BAL fluid revealed M. 
      pneumoniae-DNA. A three-wk course of macrolide therapy induced rapid recovery. We 
      conclude that M. pneumoniae infection should be considered in immunosuppressed 
      patients with long-lasting respiratory complaints and fever of unknown origin. 
      Antibiotic treatment should be given for a minimum of three wk.
