TI  - Double aortic arch with dominant left arch and right ligamentum arteriosum.
PG  - 474-7
LID - 10.1111/j.1747-0803.2009.00286.x [doi]
AB  - This case report describes a rare example of double aortic arch with a dominant 
      left aortic arch, patent minor right aortic arch, left descending aorta, and 
      right ligamentum arteriosum causing tracheobronchial compression in a twin baby 
      girl with DiGeorge syndrome. She also had large right subclavian artery arising 
      from right-sided diverticulum of Kommerell, aplastic thymus, T cell lymphopenia 
      with normal immunoglobulin, hypocalcemia, and hypomagnesemia. The diverticulum of 
      Kommerell was resected and minor right aortic arch, right ligamentum arteriosum, 
      and right subclavian artery were divided through right posterolateral 
      thoracotomy. Aortopexy was performed under bronchoscopic guidance to relieve the 
      airway compression. We strongly suggest a right-sided approach in this type of 
      vascular arrangement for easy access and better outcome.
