DP  - 2009 Oct-Dec
TI  - A 12-year-old girl with Hodgkin's disease presenting as a non-resolving pneumonia 
      and an endobronchial nodule.
PG  - 241-3
AB  - The presence of a non-resolving pneumonia warrants the suspicion of a possible 
      malignancy. While pulmonary involvement in Hodgkin's disease can present as a 
      non-resolving pneumonia, the clinical clues of dyspnoea, stridor and wheeze point 
      to a possible endobronchial involvement. A bronchoscopy in such a situation can 
      be valuable for diagnosis, and can aid in staging of the disease. The true 
      incidence of endobronchial involvement in Hodgkin's disease is not known, but 
      when diagnosed early and treated appropriately, the prognosis is usually good, 
      and a complete cure is possible.
