VI  - 47
IP  - 12
DP  - 2009 Dec
TI  - [A case of bronchogenic carcinoma which obtained long-term relapse free survival 
      by argon plasma coagulation].
PG  - 1093-7
AB  - A 69-year-old man was admitted complaining of bloody sputum. A chest X-ray and CT 
      revealed no abnormal findings, but bronchoscopy revealed a squamous cell 
      carcinoma nodular lesion in the bifurcation between the left B(1+2)a and B(1+2)b. 
      After two courses of chemotherapy (CBDCA + PAC), the nodular lesion disappeared. 
      Another nodular squamous cell carcinoma grew in the orifice of the left B(1+2) 
      nine months later, but this time chemotherapy was ineffective. The nodular tumor 
      was cauterized twice with argon plasma coagulation, and no recurrence appeared 
      for twenty-six months.
