DP  - 2009 Jun
TI  - Bronchoscopy screening in primary oral squamous cell carcinoma: a 10-year 
      experience.
PG  - 279-83
LID - 10.1016/j.bjoms.2009.01.009 [doi]
AB  - Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) in the head and neck is associated with synchronous 
      or metachronous carcinomas of the lung. Preoperative pulmonary screening is 
      advocated and may be done by bronchoscopy, thoracic radiograph, computed 
      tomography (CT), or positron emission tomography (PET) with CT (PET/CT fusion). 
      We evaluated the role of bronchoscopy in patients with primary oral SCC to 
      ascertain the incidence of synchronous malignancies of the lung. We 
      retrospectively reviewed a decade's experience of screening by bronchoscopy in 
      570 pathologically confirmed and previously untreated patients with oral SCC (188 
      female, 382 male). Univariate and multivariate analyses were done after 
      evaluating the incidence of synchronous lesions and the clinical and histological 
      features of the index tumour. Investigation by bronchoscopy showed disease in 166 
      patients, and malignancy of the lung in 9 (2%). The Union International contre le 
      Cancer (UICC) stages I and II oral SCC were significantly associated with a 
      synchronous malignancy of the lung (p=0.038). We recommend the use of 
      bronchoscopy even in early tumour stages. Some patients had their treatment 
      altered because of its use, including upstaging, diagnosis of distant and 
      unresectable disease, and investigation of second primary malignancies.
