DP  - 2009 May
TI  - Pulmonary complications of tumor necrosis factor-targeted therapy.
PG  - 661-9
LID - 10.1016/j.rmed.2009.01.002 [doi]
AB  - Tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-targeted therapies are increasingly being prescribed 
      in the management of a variety of inflammatory and autoimmune diseases. The use 
      of this class of medications also pose risks of developing an assortment of 
      pulmonary side effects including infections (TB, bacterial, and fungal 
      infections), pulmonary nodules, chronic pneumonitis/fibrosis, SLE-like reactions, 
      vasculitis, and exacerbations of underlying lung disease. In addition to 
      surveillance for tuberculosis prior to initiation of TNF-targeted therapy, a high 
      level of vigilance should be maintained during administration for infectious and 
      non-infectious complications, even years into a patient's course. The available 
      evidence argues for caution in using these agents in patients with pre-existing 
      lung disease and heightened suspicion of accelerated nodule formation in those 
      with pre-existing rheumatoid nodules. Management centers on excluding infection, 
      identifying confounders (especially methotrexate or pre-existing lung disease), 
      and promptly discontinuing TNF-targeted therapy. In some instances, invasive 
      procedures (e.g. bronchoscopy or VATS lung biopsy) will be necessary to establish 
      the proper diagnosis, and the administration of steroids may be beneficial.
