TI  - Inhalation burn injury in children.
PG  - 147-54
LID - 10.1111/j.1460-9592.2008.02884.x [doi]
AB  - With advances in burn care, many children are surviving severe burn injuries. 
      Inhalation injury remains a predictor of morbidity and mortality in burn injury. 
      Inhalation of smoke and toxic gases leads to pulmonary complications, including 
      airway obstruction from bronchial casts, pulmonary edema, decreased pulmonary 
      compliance, and ventilation-perfusion mismatch, as well as systemic toxicity from 
      carbon monoxide poisoning and cyanide toxicity. The diagnosis of inhalation 
      injury is suggested by the history and physical exam and can be confirmed by 
      bronchoscopy. Management consists of supportive measures, pulmonary toilet, 
      treatment of pulmonary infection and ventilatory support as needed. This review 
      details the pathophysiology, diagnosis, and management options for inhalation 
      injury.
