TI  - Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy secondary to bronchial adenocarcinoma and 
      coexisting pulmonary tuberculosis: a case report.
PG  - 221
LID - 10.1186/1757-1626-1-221 [doi]
AB  - A 44-year-old man presented with painful swelling of wrists and ankles, severe 
      pain at both tibiae, clubbing of fingers and toes and arthritis in wrist and 
      ankle joints. The chest roentgenogram showed consolidation of the right lower 
      lobe, whereas plain roentgenograms revealed solid periosteal reaction at both 
      tibiae. CT and bronchoscopy confirmed the presence of adenocarcinoma of the right 
      lower lobe. Moreover, mycobacterium of tuberculosis was isolated by culture of 
      the patient's sputum.Our patient received antituberculous treatment and soon he 
      underwent surgical excision of the tumour and subsequent chemotherapy. Ten months 
      later, he returned with metastatic lesions in the brain and the adrenals. A few 
      days later, he died.The patient suffered from bronchial adenocarcinoma as well as 
      pulmonary tuberculosis. As a complication of these two coexisting conditions, the 
      patient developed hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy.
