TI  - Talc granulomatosis mimicking sarcoidosis.
PG  - e168-70
AB  - Pulmonary disease due to talc, a group of hydrous magnesium silicates, is almost 
      exclusively encountered secondary to occupational exposure or intravenous drug 
      abuse. Talcosis or talc pneumoconiosis is one of the rarer forms of 
      silicate-induced lung disease. It is seen in workers exposed during its 
      production, and occasionally, in users of cosmetic talc and in intravenous drug 
      addicts. Very often, the history of exposure is not recognised by the patient, 
      and it is only the finding of granulomatous cellular interstitial lesions 
      containing birefringent crystals which indicates considerable talc exposure. We 
      report a 38-year-old woman who was initially diagnosed with sarcoidosis, until a 
      bronchoscopic biopsy revealed the presence of numerous foreign body giant cells 
      and birefringent particles forming non-caseating granulomas. There was no history 
      of occupational exposure to talc or intravenous drug abuse. The patient responded 
      to oral corticosteroid treatment. Talcosis is generally considered to be 
      relatively benign.
