TI  - Pulmonary capillary bleeding in a patient with severe left ventricular failure 
      after acute myocardial infarction under anti-thrombotic therapy.
PG  - 721-6
AB  - A 52-year-old man who was admitted for cardiogenic shock after acute myocardial 
      infarction developed severe left ventricular dysfunction despite reperfusion 
      therapy with coronary stents. After the 40th hospital day, he started to have 
      cough and pulmonary infiltrates. Antimicrobial therapies and subsequent 
      prednisolone for bronchiolitis obliterans organizing pneumonia were ineffective. 
      Bronchoscopic examination revealed diffuse pulmonary bleeding and exudation of 
      hemosiderin-containing macrophages in bronchial lavage fluid. Pulmonary capillary 
      bleeding has been reported in the terminal stage of patients with mitral stenosis 
      in the pre-cardiac surgery era. This complication reemerges in patients with 
      severe heart failure receiving intensive anti-coagulation therapy after 
      implanting a sirolimus-eluting coronary stent.
