IS  - 0012-0472 (Linking)
VI  - 131
IP  - 40
DP  - 2006 Oct 6
TI  - [Bronchoscopic diagnosis of peripheral pulmonary foci].
PG  - 2229-33
AB  - Flexible fibreoptic bronchoscopy is frequently performed in patients with 
      endoscopically not visible lesions, especially for establishing a diagnosis in 
      patients with peripheral lung cancer. The usual method for obtaining material for 
      histological analysis is transbronchial forceps biopsy. To gain material for 
      cytological examination transbronchial needle aspiration, transbronchial catheter 
      aspiration or bronchial brushing and bronchial washing can be used. The size of 
      the lesion and its location influence the diagnostic accuracy of bronchoscopy. 
      The reliability of cytological procedures has found general acceptance: the 
      endoscopic investigation can be guided by the results of on-site cytology. The 
      combination of transbronchial forceps biopsy and cytological methods increases 
      the diagnostic yield. In a direct comparison, cytological examination of material 
      from transbronchial needle aspiration and transbronchial catheter aspiration has 
      been found to have a significantly higher diagnostic sensitivity than 
      transbronchial forceps biopsy in peripheral bronchial carcinoma. While 
      transbronchial needle aspiration makes it possible to obtain tissue from 
      extrabronchial region, catheter aspiration provides material of a larger area of 
      the bronchial mucosa. There is thus no need for an exact positioning of the 
      catheter tip inside the lesion under fluoroscopic guidance.
