TI  - Consecutive left lower sleeve lobectomy and left S3 segmentectomy for a patient 
      with node-negative double lung cancer.
PG  - 342-4
AB  - A 74-year-old-man visited our hospital because of a dry cough. A chest radiograph 
      showed a nodular shadow measuring 2.0 cm diameter in the left S3 segment and a 
      tumor shadow measuring 3.5 cm diameter in the left S6 segment; no mediastinal 
      lymph node enlargement was observed. The bronchoscopic findings revealed direct 
      invasion of the tumor into the spur of the left B6 branch, but no abnormal 
      findings were found in the upper bronchus. An endobronchial biopsy revealed 
      squamous cell carcinoma in a left B6 biopsy specimen and adenocarcinoma in the 
      left S3 lung biopsy specimen. The patient's lung function was not good, and an 
      arterial blood gas analysis was Po2 69.3mmHg and PCO2 48.5 mmHg. We performed 
      left lower sleeve lobectomy and left S3 segmentectomy simultaneously. He was 
      discharged uneventfully on the 14th day after the operation, and he has since 
      been doing fine without lung caner recurrence for 3 years after surgery.
