DP  - 2006 Mar-Apr
TI  - Tracheal tear and tension pneumothorax complicating bronchoscopy-guided 
      percutaneous tracheostomy.
PG  - 144-5
AB  - Percutaneous dilatational tracheostomy (PDT) is a frequently conducted procedure 
      in critically ill patients. Bronchoscopic guidance of PDT is generally 
      recommended to minimize the risk of unintentional tracheal injury. We present a 
      case of tracheal tear and tension pneumothorax, a rare but potentially 
      life-threatening complication, during continuously bronchoscopy-guided PDT. 
      Sealing the large tracheal air fistula with the cuff of an endotracheal tube 
      helped bridge time to definitive surgical repair in our patient. Bronchoscopic 
      guidance may minimize, but cannot completely eliminate, the risk of tracheal 
      injury during PDT.
