DP  - 2004 Oct
TI  - [A case of pulmonary nocardiosis with a polypoid lesion in a bronchus].
PG  - 893-6
AB  - A 62-year-old woman was admitted with fever and bloody sputum. A mass shadow in 
      the left S3 and obstruction of the left B3 were seen on a chest radiograph and 
      CT. Obstructive pneumonia was suspected, and cefotiam and imipenem/cilastatin 
      were administered. However, this treatment did not show adequate efficacy. 
      Bronchoscopy demonstrated a yellowish-white polypoid lesion in the left B3, but 
      histopathological findings with HE staining yielded no definite diagnosis. 
      Subsequently, Nocardia asteroides was detected in sputum test. A 
      sulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim combination and minocycline were administered, and 
      the clinical findings improved. Gram-positive microfilaments were confirmed 
      retrospectively in the pathologic specimen, and a diagnosis of pulmonary 
      nocardiosis was made.
