VI  - 1
IP  - 3
DP  - 2000 Sep
TI  - Atelectasis: mechanisms, diagnosis and management.
PG  - 274-8
AB  - The term atelectasis describes a state of collapsed and non-aerated region of the 
      lung parenchyma, which is otherwise normal. This pathological condition is 
      usually associated with several pulmonary and chest disorders and represents a 
      manifestation of the underlying disease, not a disease per se. Atelectasis may 
      occur in three ways: (i) airway obstruction; (ii) compression of parenchyma by 
      extrathoracic, intrathoracic, chest wall processes; and (iii) increased surface 
      tension in alveoli and bronchioli. Chest radiographs using both the 
      anterior-posterior and lateral projections are mandatory to document the presence 
      of atelectasis. Differentiation from lobar consolidation may be a clinical 
      dilemma. The treatment of atelectasis varies depending on duration and severity 
      of the causal disease from chest physiotherapy to postural drainage, 
      bronchodilator and anti-inflammatory therapy. Persistent mucous plugs should be 
      removed by bronchoscopy.
