TI  - Current concepts in the staging of non-small cell lung cancer.
PG  - 181-90
AB  - When evaluating a patient diagnosed with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), 
      staging helps define the extent of disease. The main goals of staging are to 
      assist in determining appropriate treatment options (surgery versus non-surgical) 
      and in predicting prognosis. Based on the recommendation of the American Joint 
      Committee for Cancer (AJCC), a TNM (tumor, node, and metastases) staging system 
      is used for NSCLC. Clinical staging (cTNM) is determined using non-invasive 
      techniques such as clinical assessment and radiologic testing. Pathologic staging 
      (pTNM) is determined using invasive techniques such as bronchoscopy, 
      mediastinoscopy, and video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery, or at the time of 
      thoracotomy. Recently, new staging modalities such as positron emission 
      tomography and intraoperative sentinel node mapping have been used with promising 
      results. In the near future, these techniques, as well as molecular and serum 
      tumor markers, will likely be used to more accurately determine the burden of 
      disease and allow for more appropriate treatment.
