TI  - TB or not TB: an unusual sore finger.
PG  - 490-1
AB  - A 25 year old Afro-Carribean presented to the accident and emergency department 
      with a painful swelling of the little finger. Radiology revealed periosteal 
      elevation and a bone scan showed a hot spot in the proximal phalanx, suggestive 
      of a tumour or an infection. He gave a history of recurrent chest infections in 
      the past, the chest radiograph revealed apical lesions and loculated pleural 
      effusion. Bronchoscopy washings were positive for acid fast bacillus suggestive 
      of pulmonary tuberculosis with tuberculous dactylitis. A detailed history and 
      high index of suspicion is important as delay in diagnosis can have serious 
      consequences for the patient.
