DP  - 2000 Nov
TI  - [Unexpected difficult intubation caused by a laryngeal web].
PG  - 1278-80
AB  - We report a case of unexpected difficult intubation in an adult caused by a 
      laryngeal web. A 43-year-old woman with uterine myoma was scheduled for abdominal 
      hysterectomy. After induction of anesthesia, the vocal cords were seen clearly 
      under laryngoscopy. Although intubation was attempted several times, a 6.5 mm 
      internal diameter tracheal tube could not be passed below the level of the vocal 
      cords because of resistance, and we used a laryngeal mask during anesthesia. Next 
      morning after the operation, she developed dyspnea. Bronchoscopy revealed a very 
      narrow airway below the level of vocal cords caused by a laryngeal web. 
      Tracheostomy was performed. Two weeks later, tracheostomy was closed without any 
      sequela.
