TI  - Transient facial nerve palsy after general anesthesia: A case report.
PG  - 443-445
LID - 10.4097/kjae.2009.56.4.443 [doi]
AB  - A 44-year-old woman underwent a breast cancer operation under general anesthesia. 
      We performed bronchoscope guided intubation along with a jaw thrust maneuver on 
      the patient to achieve a good visual field. The patient complained of left lower 
      lip weakness after the operation. The lower lip of the patient was deviated to 
      the right. The cause might have been a temporary compression of the left facial 
      nerve at the mandibular angle during performance of the jaw thrust maneuver. 
      Fortunately, the patient's palsy recovered completely during the following one 
      month. We report here on a case of transient facial nerve palsy after general 
      anesthesia, and this occurred despite performing a very short duration of jaw 
      thrust.
