IS  - 1752-1947 (Linking)
VI  - 2
DP  - 2008 May 22
TI  - Bronchial obstruction secondary to idiopathic scoliosis in a child: a case 
      report.
PG  - 171
LID - 10.1186/1752-1947-2-171 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Patients with severe idiopathic scoliosis are reported to have 
      significant pulmonary complications, including recurrent chest infections, 
      alveolar hypoventilation and respiratory failure. CASE PRESENTATION: We report a 
      case of a 13-year-old boy with moderate-to-severe scoliosis resulting in torsion 
      or twisting of the bronchus intermedius, which contributed to airflow obstruction 
      defects, as revealed by both spirometry and bronchoscopy. CONCLUSION: We 
      recommend that inspection of the shape of the maximal expiratory flow-volume loop 
      obtained from spirometry, as well as other parameters suggestive of obstructive 
      lung disease, may be important in children with scoliosis. To the best of the 
      authors' knowledge, this is the first report of a child in which pulmonary 
      function testing and direct visualization via a flexible bronchoscope have been 
      used to characterize intrathoracic large airway obstruction.
