TI  - Multimodality imaging of tracheobronchial disorders in children.
PG  - e29
LID - 10.1148/rg.e29 [doi]
AB  - The trachea and bronchial airways in children are subject to compromise by a 
      number of extrinsic and intrinsic conditions, including congenital, inflammatory, 
      infectious, traumatic, and neoplastic processes. Stridor, wheezing, and 
      respiratory distress are the most common indications for imaging of the airway in 
      children. Frontal and lateral chest and/or neck radiography constitute the 
      initial investigations of choice in most cases. Options for additional imaging 
      include airway fluoroscopy, contrast esophagography, computed tomography (CT), 
      and magnetic resonance (MR) imaging. Advanced imaging techniques such as dynamic 
      airway CT, CT angiography, MR angiography, and cine MR imaging are valuable for 
      providing relevant vascular and functional information in certain settings. 
      Postprocessing techniques such as multiplanar reformatting, volume rendering, and 
      virtual bronchoscopy assist in surgical planning by providing a better 
      representation of three-dimensional anatomy. A systematic approach to imaging the 
      airway based on clinical symptoms and signs is essential for the prompt, safe, 
      and accurate diagnosis of tracheobronchial disorders in children.
