TI  - Role of CD8 lymphocytes and neutrophilic alveolitis in Pneumocystis jiroveci 
      pneumonia.
PG  - 612-4
AB  - We described the characteristics of bronchoalveolar inflammatory cells and their 
      correlation with lung injury in patients with Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia. We 
      reviewed all cases of patients with Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia in newly 
      diagnosed HIV infected patients admitted to a large metropolitan referral 
      hospital during June 2003 to December 2004. Nine patients (5M, 4F) with 
      Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia diagnosed with bronchoscopy and cytological 
      examination of bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) were identified. There was a positive 
      correlation between peripheral CD8 count and BAL neutrophilia and negative 
      correlation with hypoxemia. Although the number patients in this case series is 
      small, our findings suggest that CD8 cells and alveolar neutrophilic inflammation 
      have a role in lung injury in Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia. These findings are 
      consistent with data from animal studies.
