TI  - Video assisted thoracoscopic surgery in patients with small peripheral pulmonary 
      tumors.
PG  - 273-5
AB  - PURPOSE: Despite the existence of many diagnostic methods such as bronchoscopy, 
      computerized tomographic (CT) scan, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) and 
      transthoracic aspiration biopsy, small peripheral pulmonary lesions cannot always 
      be verified. With this communication we describe our experience with the 
      video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) in the diagnosis and treatment of 
      small peripheral pulmonary tumors. PATIENTS AND METHODS: The study included 22 
      patients evaluated and treated with VATS because of small peripheral lung 
      lesions. The patients were operated on at the Clinic of Thoracoabdominal Surgery 
      of the Medical University, Plovdiv, during a period of 6 years (1997-2002). 
      RESULTS: Pulmonary hamartoma was found in 8 patients, solitary metastasis in 8, 
      primary lung cancer in 4 and tuberculoma in 2. Twenty patients underwent partial 
      pulmonary resection using VATS, and in 2 patients VATS was turned into 
      thoracotomy, because the tumor was situated deeply in the pulmonary parenchyma 
      and was not found with VATS. In 4 of the patients thoracotomy and lobectomy were 
      performed because of histological confirmation of primary lung cancer. 
      CONCLUSION: VATS is a safe, effective and cost-effective method for the diagnosis 
      and treatment of peripheral pulmonary tumors.
