VI  - 120
IP  - 11
DP  - 2006 Nov
TI  - Multiple laryngeal foreign bodies composed of dried fish vertebral bones: a case 
      report.
PG  - 976-8
AB  - We present a rare case of tracheostomy for removal of laryngeal foreign bodies 
      consisting of three connected fish vertebral bones in a 15-month-old girl. Recent 
      endoscopic techniques have made it possible to extract nearly all 
      tracheobronchial foreign bodies with rigid bronchoscopes. However, the three 
      connected foreign bodies in this report could not be extracted entirely by single 
      endoscopy because the glottis as an exit was narrow due to severe oedema. 
      Accordingly, tracheostomy was required to assist ventilation, prevent prolonged 
      post-operative endotracheal intubation, remove the secondary tracheal foreign 
      bodies and to provide a conduit for the introduction of a bronchoscope. This 
      suggests that tracheostomy should be considered to avoid the potential dangers of 
      severe laryngeal oedema and to secure the route for removal of foreign bodies 
      from the trachea when treating patients with multiple laryngeal foreign bodies 
      and laryngeal oedema.
