DP  - 2005 Oct
TI  - Polypoid endobronchial lung cyst with bronchoscopic removal: a case report.
PG  - 892-4
AB  - Pulmonary bronchogenic cyst in adults is rare and the typical appearance is a 
      sharply circumscribed, round or oval nodule or mass, usually in the medial third 
      of the lungs. Bronchial polyps are rare histopathologically distinct 
      nonneoplastic endobronchial lesions and are classified as multiple papillomas, 
      solitary papillomas, and inflammatory polyps. We herein report a patient with 
      polypoid endobronchial lung cyst. A 68-yr-old woman presented with a discomfort 
      and pain in the right upper chest of four weeks' duration. Chest radiography 
      revealed a cystic lesion in the right upper lung. Computed tomography revealed a 
      4 x 5 cm sized large cyst. Neither enlarged mediastinal lymph nodes nor 
      extrabronchial involvements were observed. Flexible bronchoscopy revealed a 
      peduncular polyp about 2 cm in length originating from the anterior segment of 
      right upper lung. After bronchoscopic removal of polyp, cystic lesion of the 
      right upper lung disappeared.
