TI  - Awake nasotracheal intubation using fiberoptic bronchoscope in a pediatric 
      patient with Freeman-Sheldon syndrome.
PG  - 790-2
AB  - The Freeman-Sheldon syndrome is a congenital disease primarily affecting the 
      facial, limb and respiratory muscles that give rise to classical clinical 
      features including typical whistling face and short webbed neck associated with 
      difficult intubation. We present successful awake nasotracheal intubation in a 
      6-year-old patient with typical clinical features of Freeman-Sheldon syndrome by 
      using fiberoptic bronchoscope on two separate occasions.
