TI  - [Successful implantation of a metal coronary angioplasty stent for bronchomalacia 
      of the right tracheal bronchus associated with right isomerism complex in an 
      early infant].
PG  - 537-41
AB  - One-month-old boy had severe emphysema of the right upper lobe due to the 
      stenotic tracheal bronchus compressed between the distorted right patent ductus 
      arteriosus (PDA) and the right aortic arch associated with right isomerism 
      complex. He underwent a left modified Blalock-Taussig shunt and a division of the 
      PDA on cardiopulmonary bypass. Extracorporeal lung support (ECLS) was introduced 
      because of severe hypoxemia caused by remaining bronchomalacia of the tracheal 
      bronchus. On postoperative day 3, a metal coronary angioplasty stent was 
      implanted at the stenotic lesion under fluoroscopic and bronchoscopic guidance. 
      He was successfully weaned from ECLS and then respirator after the implantation. 
      This simple stenting procedure might be an effective alternate in the treatment 
      of bronchomalacia or bronchial stenosis in early infancy.
