TI  - [Clinical analysis of pulmonary actinomycosis].
PG  - 111-6
AB  - A clinical analysis of four patients diagnosed as pulmonary actinomycosis in our 
      respiratory division during the last seven years was performed. All of the 
      patients were males with an average age of 61 years. Three patients had a past 
      history. The clinical diagnosis on admission was lung cancer or pulmonary 
      suppuration in three patients showing a mass-like shadow and pneumonia in one 
      patient with an infiltration shadow. Suspected pulmonary infectious disease was 
      detected from clinical symptoms in two patients, while suspected lung cancer was 
      detected in the other two patients during health examinations. The lesions 
      dominantly existed in the right upper lobe. Such findings as central low 
      attenuation (LAA), bronchiectasis and pleural thickening were characteristic on 
      chest computed tomography (CT). The diagnostic methods were all negative 
      procedures; video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) in two patients, a 
      specimen obtained by bronchoscopy in one and a specimen taken by percutaneous 
      aspiration in one. Because penicillin antibiotics were administered for a long 
      time for all patients after obtaining a definite diagnosis, the prognosis was 
      good and there were no relapse.
