TI  - [A case of pulmonary actinomycosis, who expectorated sulfur granules, caused by 
      Actinomyces odontolyticus and Actinomyces meyeri].
PG  - 231-5
AB  - A 51-year-old man complaining of cough and bloody sputum, was admitted to our 
      hospital because of antibiotic-resistant chronic pneumonia in the right upper 
      lobe. Initially, bronchoscopic examination and sputum culture revealed no 
      evidence of malignancy or any specific infection, either pathologically or 
      microbiologically. However, pathological examination of a solid body expectorated 
      with sputum revealed typical sulfur granules, indicating pulmonary actinomycosis. 
      Two actinomyceses named Actinomyces odontolyticus and Actinomyces meyeri were 
      detected later. Pulmonary infection caused by these types of actinomyceses is 
      rare, and the diagnostic procedure seemed to be unusual.
