TI  - [Tuberculosis induced by Bacillus Calmette-Guerin immuno-prophylaxis -- case 
      study].
PG  - 773-6
AB  - We describe a case of miliary tuberculosis induced by Bacillus Calmette-Guerin 
      (BCG) as a complication of an infection after BCG-instillation therapy into the 
      bladder because of bladder carcinoma. Bacilli surely entered blood circulation 
      via an urethral lesion because of a difficult catheterisation. The 60 year old 
      patient was administered to the hospital with septic temperature 4 four days 
      after BCG instillation. CT showed a miliary patten and the diagnosis was 
      confirmed by bronchoscopy: transbronchial biopsy showed granulomatous 
      infiltration and an acid-fast rod-bacterium was detected in bronchial slime. 
      Symptoms vanished after a consequent antituberculous triple therapy regime and 
      the patient left hospital in a good general state of health.
