VI  - 68
IP  - 11
DP  - 2004 Nov
TI  - Failure to thrive caused by the coexistence of vallecular cyst, laryngomalacia 
      and gastroesophageal reflux in an infant.
PG  - 1459-64
AB  - Vallecular cyst is a rare but dangerous cause of stridor in neonates and young 
      infants. Without recognition and proper therapy, the resulting airway obstruction 
      can lead to serious morbidity and mortality. We herein report a young infant with 
      a vallecular cyst synchronous with laryngomalacia and gastroesophageal reflux. 
      The infant initially presented with signs and symptoms mimicking more benign 
      laryngomalacia. Diagnosis was delayed with accompanying failure to thrive. The 
      cyst was revealed by a barium esophagogram and confirmed by flexible 
      bronchoscopy. Laryngomicrosurgery with CO(2) laser not only removed the cyst, but 
      resolved the co-existing laryngomalacia and gastroesophageal reflux as well. 
      After surgery, the infant was symptom free and exhibited a steady weight gain. 
      There was no recurrence during 1 year of follow-up. We have reviewed the 
      literature for this rare and potentially devastating lesion.
