TI  - Pulmonary cystic hydatid disease in Ireland.
PG  - 204-5
AB  - BACKGROUND: Hydatid disease is rare in Ireland and its incidence and prevalence 
      are unknown. Most cases are diagnosed by a combination of clinical findings, 
      morphological features on imaging and by serological testing. AIMS: We describe 
      an Irish case of pulmonary hydatid disease detected at bronchoscopy by 
      bronchoalveolar lavage, and discuss the diagnosis and treatment of the disorder. 
      CONCLUSION: The diagnosis of this rare disease requires a high index of 
      suspicion. Treatment is primarily surgical, with a role for antihelminthic 
      agents.
