TI  - [Hyponatraemic delirium as an early symptom of small-cell bronchial carcinoma].
PG  - 1016-9
AB  - In the psychiatric clinic, delirious states are rare as the first symptoms of 
      paraneoplastic syndrome, and the aetiology can only be clarified by consequent 
      differential diagnostic efforts. We report the case of a 49-year-old man who was 
      admitted to our psychiatric unit with delirium. Laboratory investigations showed 
      hyponatraemia, serum hypo-osmolality, and urine hyperosmolality characterising 
      the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuresis (SIADH). As a paraneoplastic syndrome, 
      SIADH is most frequently associated with small-cell lung cancer (SCLC). Whereas 
      chest X-ray was negative, chest CT scan and bronchoscopy including histology 
      revealed a SCLC at an early stage of limited disease. Early oncological therapy 
      made it possible for our patient to return to work with a satisfactory quality of 
      life. Twenty-one months after first admission, he died as a result of recurrent 
      SCLC metastases.
