TI  - Delayed diagnosis of foreign body aspiration in children.
PG  - 100-1
AB  - Foreign body aspiration in children is common and usually presents with an 
      initial episode of choking with subsequent respiratory symptoms. There may be 
      cough, wheeze, or stridor, with decreased or abnormal breath sounds on 
      examination. However, it can mimic other illnesses and cause difficulty in 
      diagnosis. Radiological investigations may help to confirm aspiration but should 
      not be used to exclude it. Three cases are presented of foreign body aspiration 
      with a delay in diagnosis ranging from days to weeks. It is believed that delay 
      could have been avoided with a more careful approach to the history and more 
      appropriate use of investigations. These cases demonstrate that children with a 
      history of choking and subsequent symptoms should be referred for bronchoscopy.
