TI  - A technique for thoracoscopic aortopericardiosternopexy.
PG  - 1639
AB  - BACKGROUND: A left thoracotomy is the standard access for aortosternopexy in 
      severe tracheomalacia. We report a modified technique for thoracoscopic 
      aortopericardiosternopexy. METHODS: The thymus is mobilized, and the needle is 
      passed through the sternum and back. In extensive or recurrent tracheomalacia, 
      not only the ascending aorta but also the innominate artery and pericardial base 
      are fixed to the sternum. The effect is monitored bronchoscopically. RESULTS: 
      This technique showed dramatic success in two children, one 4-year-old and a 
      2-year-old. In the younger child, the thoracoscopy was a redo procedure after a 
      previous open aortosternopexy. CONCLUSIONS: Thoracoscopic 
      aortopericardiosternopexy is an effective procedure that does not impair 
      postoperative respiration. It should therefore be considered for severe 
      tracheomalacia or even redo operations.
