DP  - 2002 May-Jun
TI  - Inverted papilloma of the nasal cavity presenting with massive amounts of 
      squamous metaplastic cells in sputum. A case report.
PG  - 596-600
AB  - BACKGROUND: Squamous metaplasic cells are rarely seen in sputum of female 
      nonsmokers. CASE: A 47-year-old female nonsmoker presented with massive amounts 
      of squamous metaplasic cells in sputum and an elevated level of squamous cell 
      carcinoma (SCC) antigen in serum present for months, while no causative lesion 
      was detected either by lung computed tomography or bronchoscopy. The patient was 
      eventually diagnosed as having inverted papilloma in the right nasal cavity. 
      Resection of the tumor brought about disappearance of squamous metaplastic cells 
      in sputum and return of serum SCC antigen to the normal range. CONCLUSION: This 
      case clearly demonstrates that squamous metaplastic cells in sputum can originate 
      in lesions in the nasal cavity, although they are rare. It should be kept in mind 
      that the nasal cavity is a potential site producing squamous metaplastic cells in 
      sputum.
