TI  - Left bronchial disruption and aortic rupture after blunt chest trauma.
PG  - 382-3
AB  - A 25-year-old male who had been involved in a traffic accident presented with a 
      neurological disorder, bilateral pneumothoraces, and pneumomediastinum. 
      Bronchoscopy revealed a complex rupture of the left bronchial tract. MRI revealed 
      a sinus valsalva aneurysm. The bronchial lesion was first repaired via left 
      thoracotomy. 10 days later, the aorta was repaired via sternotomy. In cases of 
      combined bronchial and aortic lesion, a concomitant repair is not mandatory, at 
      least when the aortic lesion appears limited and shows no signs of dissection.
