TI  - Paradoxical vocal cord adduction mimicking as acute asthma in a pediatric 
      patient.
PG  - 55-8
AB  - We report an adolescent girl with paradoxical vocal cord adduction who presented 
      with acute onset of hyperventilation, wheezing and stridor that did not respond 
      to bronchodilator and anti-inflammation therapy. The paradoxical vocal cord 
      motion was confirmed by flexible fiberoptic bronchoscopic examination. We found 
      the stridor was induced by hyperventilation, and was caused by paradoxical vocal 
      cord movement. The abnormal cord motion may be psychogenic and could be 
      misdiagnosed as asthma. It is important to investigate the underlying background 
      and social history and to avoid unnecessary use of beta-agonists, steroids, and 
      even endotracheal intubation or tracheostomy.
