IS  - 0012-3692 (Linking)
VI  - 119
IP  - 5
DP  - 2001 May
TI  - Hepatitis B-related polyarteritis nodosa complicated by pulmonary hemorrhage.
PG  - 1608-10
AB  - A 34-year-old man presented with fever, weight loss, paresthesia, abdominal pain, 
      and hypertension. He had hepatitis B antigenemia, with negative antineutrophil 
      cytoplasmic antibody, antinuclear antibody, and antiglomerular basement membrane 
      serology results. Renal arteriography showed multiple intrarenal microaneurysms. 
      In spite of therapy with antiviral agents (lamivudine, famciclovir), prednisone, 
      cyclophosphamide, and plasmapheresis, renal function deteriorated. He later 
      developed rapidly progressive dyspnea and hemoptysis. Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage 
      was confirmed by bronchoscopy. He died of respiratory failure. The cause of 
      pulmonary hemorrhage in this case of polyarteritis nodosa is unclear, but may 
      include underlying capillaritis, cocaine-induced pulmonary hemorrhage, or 
      recurrent attacks of malignant hypertension.
