VI  - 71
IP  - 2
DP  - 2001 Feb
TI  - Fluoroscopy-assisted thoracoscopic surgery after computed tomography-guided 
      bronchoscopic barium marking.
PG  - 439-42
AB  - BACKGROUND: Small lesions of the peripheral lung have been detected more 
      frequently with the recent prevalence of computed tomography (CT). Identification 
      of these lesions is indispensable for wedge resection performed by video-assisted 
      thoracic surgery. Previous reports of marking techniques showed some failure and 
      complications. We have developed a new marking technique and herein describe the 
      efficacy of this technique: fluoroscopy-assisted thoracoscopic surgery after 
      computed tomography-guided bronchoscopic barium marking. METHODS: Twenty patients 
      underwent this procedure for 21 small peripheral pulmonary lesions approximately 
      10 mm in size. RESULTS: All the lesions were successfully marked and identified 
      during fluoroscopy-assisted thoracoscopy. They were resected with sufficient 
      margins. There were no complications related to this procedure. The pathologic 
      examination of these 21 lesions revealed primary lung cancer in 14, atypical 
      adenomatous hyperplasia in four, a metastatic tumor in one, and a benign tumor in 
      two. CONCLUSIONS: This procedure is both a reliable and minimally invasive 
      technique in thoracoscopic wedge resection for small peripheral pulmonary 
      lesions.
