TI  - Tracheobronchial injuries in childhood: review of two cases.
PG  - 521-3
AB  - Penetrating injuries of the tracheobronchial tree in children are very rare. With 
      prompt diagnosis nonoperative treatment seems to be appropriate and safe without 
      complications. Delayed diagnosis may result in surgical exploration with severe 
      complications afterwards caused by poor condition at the time of intervention. 
      Two children with penetrating tracheobronchial injuries were referred to our 
      pediatric surgical center in the last 12 years. A 10-year-old boy suffered an 
      iatrogenic penetrating injury of the tracheobronchial tree, and a 6-year-old boy 
      a direct penetrating injury of the distal trachea in an agricultural accident. 
      Cervical emphysema and bronchoscopy identified the lesion in these patients. Both 
      of them could be treated conservatively without any sequelae.
