DP  - 2000 Jul 14
TI  - Bronchial granuloma - where's the foreign body?
PG  - 215-9
AB  - Airway masses are uncommon in children. The majority of bronchial tumors are 
      granulomata secondary to an inhaled foreign body. However, other rare diseases 
      like primary bronchopulmonary tumors should always be considered in the 
      evaluation of a bronchial granuloma in children. The differential diagnosis of 
      bronchial granuloma is presented. We report a 7-year-old girl with a 3-year 
      history of recurrent cough and fevers who was found to have a bronchial granuloma 
      in the left upper lobe bronchus. The diagnosis of foreign body-related granuloma 
      was eventually made after combined and repeated rigid and flexible bronchoscopy. 
      This case highlights the need to search aggressively for a foreign body in the 
      presence of an airway granuloma in children, even in the absence of a history of 
      aspiration.
