TI  - Management of traumatic tracheobronchial separation in a teenager using a 
      fabricated extra-long endotracheal tube.
PG  - 1163-7
LID - 10.1016/j.ijporl.2009.04.012 [doi]
AB  - Tracheobronchial separation (TBS) due to blunt chest trauma in children is 
      extremely rare. We report the case of a 14-year-old boy who fell 20 feet and 
      developed respiratory distress, bilateral pneumothoraces, pneumomediastinum, and 
      subcutaneous emphysema. Computed tomography imaging at the initial institution 
      failed to detect tracheobronchial disruption, and the patient was managed 
      conservatively. The patient's worsening condition prompted bronchoscopic 
      examination which revealed complete separation of the right main bronchus from 
      the trachea. Single-lung ventilation was instituted using a fabricated extra-long 
      nasotracheal tube due to the patient's large size and mandibular fracture, and 
      the airway was primarily anastamosed with an open thoracotomy approach. The 
      clinical features of tracheobronchial separation as well as anesthetic, clinical 
      and surgical management of this rare but life-threatening injury are described.
