DP  - 2008 Nov
TI  - Fiberoptic bronchoscopy in children an audit from a tertiary care center.
PG  - 917-9
AB  - A retrospective review of 529 fiberoptic bronchoscopies was done with an aim to 
      identify conditions where it will be most useful. The common indications were 
      persistent/ recurrent pneumonia, persistent collapse, stridor and pulmonary 
      hemorrhage. The diagnostic yield was maximum when it was performed to identify 
      structural abnormalities and the yield was relatively poor in suspected drug 
      resistant tuberculosis, and interstitial lung diseases. Serious complications 
      were seen in children with pulmonary arterial hypertension. Fiberoptic 
      bronchoscopy is an important tool for management of respiratory problems but 
      should be performed with caution in children with pulmonary arterial 
      hypertension.
