VI  - 61
IP  - 6
DP  - 2008 Jun
TI  - [Reoperation for left main bronchial stump fistula developing after 
      pneumonectomy].
PG  - 466-9
AB  - We report the case of a 71-year-old male patient who underwent reoperation for 
      bronchial stump fistula developing after left pneumonectomy for adenocarcinoma of 
      the left lung (clinical stage IIB). After surgery, he developed persistent, 
      severe cough and chest X-ray films taken on the 23rd postoperative day showed a 
      drop in the air-fluid level in the left lung field, which, along with 
      bronchoscopic findings, strongly suggested the bronchial stump fistula and 
      subsequent reoperation was performed. Both superior pulmonary vein and main 
      pulmonary artery were dissected again proximally in pericardium, and the left 
      main bronchus was separated from the surrounding tissue. Bronchial stump was 
      closed with a stapler as close to the carina as possible, and additional 
      resection was performed. After reoperation, the patient had an uneventful course, 
      and was discharged in the second postoperative week. Shorter length of bronchial 
      stump may be the most important factor to prevent the bronchial stump fistula 
      developing after pneumonectomy.
