TI  - Extended resection of primary lung cancer directly invading the liver.
PG  - 619-20
LID - 10.1111/j.1440-1843.2008.01297.x [doi]
AB  - A 61-year-old woman presented with chest pain. Chest CT revealed a mass of 6 cm 
      diameter in the right lower lobe. Bronchoscopic biopsy showed squamous cell 
      carcinoma. Video-assisted thoracotomy revealed that the main tumour was directly 
      invading the liver through the diaphragm. To alleviate local symptoms and for 
      possible cure with adjuvant chemotherapy and radiotherapy, standard right lower 
      lobectomy and mediastinal dissection were carried out, followed by combined 
      resection of the diaphragm and posterior superior segmentectomy of the liver. 
      Eleven months postoperatively, the patient was alive but had a metastatic lesion 
      in the other lobe of the liver which reduced in size following chemotherapy.
