TI  - Foreign body aspiration in a boy with Prader-Willi Syndrome.
PG  - e12-4
AB  - A five-year-old boy presented with progressive weight gain with effort 
      intolerance and nocturnal symptoms suggesting obstructive sleep apnoea. A 
      clinical diagnosis of Prader-Willi Syndrome was made. As the initial radiography 
      and computed tomography suggested a foreign body, bronchoscopy was done under 
      general anaesthesia and impacted peanuts were removed from the left main 
      bronchus. His symptoms resolved instantly and the patient was asymptomatic at six 
      months follow-up. This report highlights the need to consider foreign body 
      aspiration as a cause for dyspnoea in children with Prader-Willi Syndrome. The 
      report also focuses on the need to adopt strategies that prevent foreign body 
      aspiration and choking in patients with Prader- Willi Syndrome.
