TI  - Tracheo-bronchitis associated with Crohn's disease improved on inhaled 
      corticotherapy.
PG  - 829-34
AB  - We report a case of tracheo-bronchitis in Crohn's disease. A 23-year-old Japanese 
      woman who had been diagnosed with Crohn's disease three years previously was 
      hospitalized. She had been suffering from dry cough for one month. Computed 
      tomography of the chest revealed marked thickening of the tracheal wall. 
      Bronchoscopy showed erythematous and edematous mucosa with diffuse whitish 
      granular lesions in the trachea and bronchi. The bronchial biopsy specimens 
      showed non-specific inflammatory infiltrates consisting of lymphocytes and plasma 
      cells, and hyperplasia of bronchial glands. Inhaled corticotherapy, fluticasone 
      propionate 800 microg/day, was effective for both the inflammatory mucosa and 
      thickened tracheal wall.
