TI  - Tracheal release maneuvers.
PG  - 201-10
AB  - Careful preoperative trachea "mapping" with accurate measurements should make an 
      unexpected shortfall in remaining tracheal length and mobility uncommon. 
      Bronchoscopy is the most accurate means of "mapping" the pathologic tracheal 
      anatomy. Rigid bronchoscopy using the ventilating bronchoscope is preferred over 
      flexible bronchoscopy because it provides the most accurate means of measuring 
      the length of stenotic trachea (especially when used in conjunction with rigid 
      fiberoptic telescopes), it can be used to dilate a tracheal stenosis before 
      operative repair, and it establishes a safe airway under direct vision. When the 
      anatomy of the tracheal pathology is accurately defined, specific plans for 
      tracheal resection can be established to accomplish a well-vascularized, 
      low-tension tracheal anastomosis.
