TI  - Endoluminal laser application under percutaneous cardiopulmonary support in 
      severe tracheal stenosis.
PG  - 301-3
AB  - Laser ablation under bronchoscopic guidance was conducted on 2 patients with 
      severe tracheal stenosis. Case 1 was a 57-year-old man admitted to our emergency 
      unit because asphyxia. Bronchoscopy showed the lumen occluded at the bifurcation 
      by tracheal cancer. Case 2 was a 62-year-old woman who underwent tracheostomy 
      elsewhere for respiratory failure caused by a brain contusion and was treated for 
      3 months. After transfer to our emergency unit, bronchoscopy showed severe 
      tracheal stenosis. Tracheoplasty conducted under bronchoscopy used a noncontact 
      Nd:YAG laser at an output of 10-40 W and irradiation time of 1 second per shot. 
      Total irradiation energy was 1700-1900 J. Percutaneous cardiopulmonary support 
      was used during the laser procedure due to asphyxia. All procedures were 
      completed satisfactorily and clinical symptoms improved dramatically in both 
      cases. Laser tracheoplasty under bronchoscopic guidance treated severe stenosis 
      safely and completely. Percutaneous cardiopulmonary support was very useful in 
      preventing severe respiratory failure or asphyxia during this procedure.
