TI  - Swyer-James syndrome complicated by lung abscess.
PG  - 252-6
AB  - Swyer-James syndrome, a rare disease with unilateral hyperlucent lung due to 
      bronchiolitis obliterans and pulmonary artery hypoplasia, generally develops 
      after lower respiratory tract infection during early childhood. Invasive 
      procedures, including bronchoscopy and angiography, are often necessary for a 
      definitive diagnosis. We report a 17-year-old man admitted because of cystic 
      bronchiectasis complicated by lung abscess. Chest roentgenography showed the 
      typical findings of Swyer-James syndrome. Noninvasive magnetic resonance 
      angiography was used to confirm hypoplasia of the right pulmonary artery. The 
      patient received antibiotic therapy, underwent a right lower lobectomy for the 
      lung abscess, and recovered.
