TI  - An unusual cause of haemoptysis and headache: cryptococcosis.
PG  - 129-32
AB  - Pulmonary cryptococcosis can be clinically silent in non-HIV infected patients 
      but can also present as nodules and masses on the chest radiograph, which can be 
      mistaken for tuberculosis or lung cancer. Common symptoms include fever and 
      cough, and uncommonly haemoptysis. This report illustrates a non-HIV infected 
      patient whose main complaint was haemoptysis and headache. He was diagnosed with 
      pulmonary cryptococcosis from biopsy of an endobronchial mass found on flexible 
      bronchoscopy. Disseminated cryptoccoccal infection should be considered as a 
      differential diagnosis in non-HIV infected patients presenting with haemoptysis 
      and headache. Early recognition and administration of appropriate therapy will 
      improve clinical outcome in these patients.
