DP  - 2006
TI  - [The role of fiberoptic bronchoscopy in the diagnostic algorithm of solitary 
      pulmonary nodule].
PG  - 16-20
AB  - The role of fiberoptic bronchoscopy in the diagnosis of peripheral lung nodule is 
      controversial. The aim of the study was to evaluate the results of routine 
      bronchoscopy performed in patients with peripheral lung nodule of 5-40 mm in 
      diameter. From 60 patients with peripheral lung nodule, who underwent routine 
      diagnostic fiberoptic bronchoscopy between 2003 - 2005, we selected 36 patients 
      in whom the final discrimination between malignant and benign tumor was achieved 
      based on either pathological examination or radiological criteria (lack of growth 
      within 2 years of radiological follow up). There were 22 (61%) patients with 
      malignant tumor and 14 (39%) with benign nodule. All malignant tumors were lung 
      carcinomas. In 10 of these patients we found macroscopic abnormalities during 
      bronchoscopy and in 9 of them pathologic examination of specimens collected 
      during the procedure could confirm malignant tissue. In two patients with benign 
      lung nodule a second lung lesion (in different lung lobe) which proved to be lung 
      cancer was identified during fiberoptic bronchoscopy. Sensitivity, specificity 
      and diagnostic accuracy of fiberoptic bronchoscopy in the diagnosis of peripheral 
      lung nodules were 41%, 100%, and 64% respectively. Abandoning fiberoptic 
      bronchoscopy in the diagnostic algorithm in patients with peripheral lung nodules 
      seems unjustified, although its limitations should be taken into account.
