DP  - 2005 Feb
TI  - Operative endoscopy of the airway with the old-fashioned esophageal dilators.
PG  - 718-9
AB  - Bronchial dilation is usually required to treat a number of disorders; the most 
      frequent are complications after airway surgery, including lung transplantation, 
      stenosis after radiotherapy, and compression by an extraluminal mass. The 
      procedure is performed by forcing the tip of a rigid bronchoscope through the 
      stenosis using barrels of increasing size; however, when there is a clear 
      discrepancy between the caliber of the rigid instrument and the stenosis, the 
      first step may be difficult. In such cases, we have successfully employed two 
      types of old-fashioned esophageal dilators rescued from the armamentarium of our 
      endoscopy unit.
