DP  - 2004 Apr
TI  - A rare case of Hurthle cell carcinoma with endobronchial metastasis.
PG  - 155-7
AB  - Endobronchial metastases (EBM) are frequently seen in breast, renal and colon 
      carcinomas. However, to our knowledge, only one case has ever been reported as 
      EBM secondary to Hurthle cell carcinoma (HCC) in the literature. A 57-year-old 
      woman had a bilateral total thyroidectomy for thyroid mass in 1990 that was 
      diagnosed as HCC. She was admitted to our outpatient clinic in August 1999, with 
      symptoms of cough, sputum, and right-sided pleuritic pain for the last seven 
      months. In the bronchoscopic examination, two endobronchial lesions were seen. 
      Pathological evaluation of the bronchoscopic samples was diagnosed as "Hurthle 
      cell carcinoma" of thyroid. We suggest that, although rare, HCC should be 
      considered in the differential diagnosis of the endobronchial metastasis.
