VI  - 30
IP  - 6
DP  - 2003 Jun
TI  - [A case of endobronchial squamous cell lung cancer successfully treated with 
      weekly chemotherapy of carboplatin and paclitaxel].
PG  - 841-4
AB  - A 69-year-old man had undergone low anterior resection and a right lobe resection 
      of the liver for rectum cancer and metastatic liver tumor at the age of 66 years. 
      He presented at our hospital because of an abnormal shadow on a CT chest scan, 
      which indicated a tumor shadow 2.5 cm in size in the lingular lobe and enlarged 
      hilar and mediastinal lymph nodes. A bronchoscopic tumor biopsy revealed 
      pulmonary metastasis from the rectum cancer. Bronchoscopic examination also 
      identified an endobronchial squamous cell lung cancer, which almost completely 
      obstructed the orifice of B1 and B2. We concluded that the patient had squamous 
      cell lung cancer with metastases in the mediastinal lymph nodes. He was initially 
      treated with weekly chemotherapy with carboplatin (AUC 1.25) and paclitaxel (70 
      mg/m2). The endobronchial tumor was markedly reduced in size after 2 weeks of the 
      chemotherapy. Furthermore, after 6 weeks of the chemotherapy, the tumor had 
      disappeared completely, and 11 days later, lower division segmentectomy and hilar 
      and mediastinal lymph node dissection were performed. Pathological examination 
      revealed no metastases in the lymph nodes. The patient has continued to receive 
      chemotherapy as an outpatient and has been well without recurrence of any 
      metastases for over 16 months.
