DP  - 2003 Apr
TI  - [Endotracheal neoplasm of a 16-year-old boy: Extremely rare primary localisation 
      of a B-cell non-Hodgkin's lymphoma].
PG  - 208-11
AB  - Primary neoplasms of the trachea are rare, in contrast to the larynx and the 
      bronchial tree. Most common types of secondary tracheal tumors are infiltrating 
      esophageal and thyroid cancers, or infiltrating lymph node metastasis. Systemic 
      non-Hodgkin's lymphomas may also involve the upper airway. Here we describe the 
      rare case of a primary extranodal endotracheal non-Hodgkin's lymphoma of a 
      16-year-old boy, who was presumed to have asthma and received antiasthmatic 
      therapy for some weeks. The tumor, 2.5 cm in size, caused significant tracheal 
      stenosis. Using rigid bronchoscopy, the difficult diagnosis was revealed and 
      specific therapy initiated.
