TI  - Orolaryngeal sarcoidosis presenting as obstructive sleep apnoea.
PG  - 85-90
AB  - A 53-year-old man was evaluated for snoring, dysphagia for solid foods and 
      difficulty of breathing and a polysomnographic recording was consistent with a 
      diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnoea syndrome (OSAS). A flexible fiberoptic 
      bronchoscopy (FFB) showed the presence of a nodular lesion of the posterior 
      ventral surface of the tongue strictly connected to the left lateral border of 
      the epiglottis. The biopsy specimen taken from the lesion was consistent with 
      sarcoidosis. No involvement of pulmonary parenchyma, lymph nodes or other organs 
      was recognized. After two months of steroid treatment, symptoms disappeared and 
      resolution of the nodular lesion at the FFB and normalization of the 
      polysomnographic recording were observed. This is the first report of 
      orolaryngeal sarcoidosis associated with OSAS as the only clinical presentation 
      of the disease.
