DP  - 2001 May
TI  - Bronchoscopically administered recombinant human DNase for lobar atelectasis in 
      cystic fibrosis.
PG  - 383-8
AB  - Lobar atelectasis is a common complication of cystic fibrosis. The majority of 
      cases respond to intravenous antibiotics and chest physiotherapy. In a subgroup 
      of patients, atelectasis is resistant to medical therapy, and its persistence in 
      the pediatric population is associated with a poor prognosis. Bronchoscopic 
      instillation of human recombinant DNase expanded atelectatic lobes in three 
      children resistant to at least 2 weeks of medical therapy. This method of 
      administration of DNase has been successful in resistant cases of lobar 
      atelectasis in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, quadriplegia, 
      and status asthmaticus. Purulent cystic fibrosis sputum has a very high DNA 
      content, and DNA has been shown to become more pourable in vitro when treated 
      with rhDNase. Bronchoscopic instillation of rhDNase should be considered in cases 
      of persistent lobar atelectasis unresponsive to medical therapy.
