VI  - 56
IP  - 1
DP  - 2001 Jan
TI  - Ultrastructural examination of bronchial biopsy specimens from children with 
      moderate asthma.
PG  - 25-9
AB  - BACKGROUND: Few studies have evaluated the asthmatic airway in childhood. The aim 
      of this study was to assess the histopathological changes occurring in the 
      bronchi of children with moderate asthma using light and electron microscopy. 
      METHODS: Bronchial biopsy specimens from 10 children with moderate asthma (seven 
      boys) of mean (SD) age 9.3 (3.8) years (range 5-14) were examined by light and 
      electron microscopy. Patients had not had a respiratory infection for at least 
      one month and they had not been treated with steroids or sodium cromoglycate for 
      four weeks before the study. Bronchoscopy was performed under general anaesthesia 
      using a Karl Storz rigid paediatric bronchoscope. Biopsy materials were stained 
      with uracyl acetate and lead citrate and evaluated under a Zeiss-10 electron 
      microscope and light microscope. RESULTS: The most important finding was 
      thickening and hyalinization of the basement membrane in nine patients. The 
      ciliated epithelial cells showed loss of cilia in some cases. Overactive 
      fibroblasts were consistently found. Six patients had degranulating mast cells 
      and lymphocyte infiltration in the submucosa. Eosinophils were seen in only one 
      biopsy sample. CONCLUSION: Children with moderate asthma develop bronchial 
      inflammation similar to the reaction observed in adults. However, in our study 
      the inflammation was rich in lymphocytes rather than eosinophils.
