DP  - 2000 Aug
TI  - Varicella-zoster virus detection by polymerase chain reaction using 
      bronchoalveolar lavage specimens.
PG  - 753-4
AB  - We report the use of polymerase chain reaction (PCR) techniques from a 
      bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) specimen for the successful early diagnosis in a 
      case of atypical but severe varicella-zoster virus (VZV) pneumonitis. Atypical 
      presentations of disseminated VZV frequently prolong the time required for 
      accurate diagnosis, resulting in increased morbidity, or mortality. Although 
      further investigation is necessary to determine the sensitivity and positive 
      predictive value of this test, PCR analysis of bronchoscopic specimens may 
      expedite the diagnosis of disseminated VZV.
